parts.stem:
Proline was first synthesised in the laboratory using a multi-stage synthetic route.

In stage 1, $\text{CH}_2(\text{CO}_2\text{C}_2\text{H}_5)_2$ and $\text{CH}_2\text{=CHCN}$ react to form a single product $\mathbf{U}$.

{{figure:pdf_visual_05.png}}

Stage 3 takes place in the presence of an acid catalyst.

$\mathbf{X}$ and $\mathbf{Y}$ are the only products of the reaction.

{{figure:pdf_visual_07.png}}

After several further stages, $\mathbf{Z}$ is produced.

{{figure:pdf_visual_08.png}}

In the final stage of the synthesis, $\mathbf{Z}$ reacts via a nucleophilic substitution mechanism to form proline.

parts.text:
Complete the diagram to describe the reaction mechanism of the final stage. Draw curly arrows, ions and charges, partial charges and lone pairs of electrons, as appropriate.

Draw the structure of any organic intermediate ion.

